{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["Mishrat anavim — Menahem explained it from וְשָׁרַת אֶת אָחִיו, and the mem is like the mem of מִרְמָס and of מִשְׁמָר and of מִשְׁמָן בְּשָׂרוֹ יָרְזֶה. But the language of sherut (service) is not applicable except to the service of human beings to their masters. Therefore I say: mishrat, from the form מְחְטוֹפֵי, is a learned verb from the root שרה, like מִקְנַת כֶּסֶף — from the root קנה. Though its participle is not found in Scripture, it is found in the language of the Talmud: a Nazirite who soaked (שרה) his bread in wine. And if in the language of Scripture — we do find in Jeremiah: אִם לֹא שִׁרִיתִיךָ לְטוֹב, and its meaning is like מִיַשְׁרָא קִטְרִין in Daniel, that is to say: I released you from the fetters by the deed that occurred. And he said שִׁרִיתִיךָ like וְזָרִיתִי פֹּרֶשׁ עַל פְּנֵיכֶם — from the root זרה that follows. So too here, mishrat anavim — everything that separates off and falls from the grapes, like a person who separates and leaps free from the bond of chains and fetters in which he was bound."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The form מחטופי appears to be a grammatical term for a learned or derived verb form; translated literally as 'from the form מחטופי' since the term is unusual and may carry a technical sense in medieval Hebrew grammatical terminology that is not entirely certain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:49:52.871623357Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}